// Durability contracts for recovery-critical state.
//
// Why this exists separately from reliability-gates.jsonc: that manifest disciplines *claims* —
// it will not let a gate assert protection without a real test, command, and passed evidence run.
// It never inspects source, so a subsystem can carry a formal invariant, eleven assertion refs and
// a green evidence run while its writes have no durability barrier at all. That is not
// hypothetical: terminal-history.daemon-checkpoint-session-isolation described "durable-history
// checkpoints" three times, and the checkpoint write had no fsync anywhere in src/main/daemon.
//
// A durability contract answers a question no gate asks: if this state is lost, what breaks, and
// what does the write owe as a result? Tiers exist because durability must be proportional —
// forcing fsync on regenerable caches buys nothing and costs an I/O storm.
//
// tiers:
//   crash-durable   The state is the only copy of something the user would notice losing, and the
//                   pointer naming it is itself fsynced. Writes must route through
//                   src/main/durable-file-write.ts — or, where the state is a credential or an
//                   identity that also needs the 0600/ACL publish, through the durable variants of
//                   src/shared/secure-file.ts, which fsync the staged handle before the rename —
//                   and carry a syscall-order proof.
//   atomic-only     Readers must never observe a torn file, but losing the newest write to power
//                   loss is acceptable because a higher tier can reconstruct it.
//   regenerable     Recomputable from a source of truth. No barrier owed.
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "policy": {
    "tiers": ["crash-durable", "atomic-only", "regenerable"],
    // Why proof is required only at the top tier: an ordering claim is the one property that cannot
    // be read off the implementation, because an fsync moved after the rename still fsyncs a file.
    "proofRequiredForTiers": ["crash-durable"],
    // Why a second clause at the same tier: the barrier question ("did the bytes reach disk") and
    // the failure question ("what is the caller told when they did not") are independent, and a
    // contract can satisfy the first while its caller turns ENOSPC into null, false or a success.
    // Ratcheted through config/durability-failure-semantics-baseline.txt, which may only shrink.
    "failureSemanticsRequiredForTiers": ["crash-durable"],
    "durablePrimitive": "src/main/durable-file-write.ts",
    // Ratcheted, not reported: config/durability-unclassified-baseline.txt grandfathers the
    // publishers that predate this gate and may only shrink, so legacy debt stays visible while a
    // newly added unclassified publisher fails the gate. "There were already some" is not an
    // argument a future change can make.
    "unclassifiedWriterPolicy": "ratchet"
  },
  "contracts": [
    {
      "id": "runtime.e2ee-host-identity",
      "state": "<userData>/orca-e2ee-keypair.json — the curve25519 keypair whose public half IS this host's fleet identity, via sha256(publicKey) in src/main/runtime/host-key-fingerprint.ts",
      "writer": "src/main/runtime/e2ee-keypair.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "Every paired device at once. The key was published by a rename with no fsync, so a power loss could leave a zero-length file; the loader then caught the parse failure and minted a fresh keypair, which changes the fingerprint. Each paired device holds a shared secret derived from the old public key and a device registry keyed to a host that no longer exists, so pairing fails with nothing to explain it. The loader now refuses a file it cannot use instead of replacing it, and discarding the identity is an explicit act (forgetHostIdentity).",
      "onFailure": "Three answers, and none replaces the key. A mint whose durable write fails throws the storage error (code intact); no in-memory key is returned, so a failed write cannot become an unpersisted identity, and pairing reports e2ee_key_unavailable with the check-writability guidance. A file that parses as invalid throws E2EEKeypairUnusableError (damaged: re-pair is the explicit recovery). A file that could not be READ — a lock, a permission, an I/O error — throws E2EEKeypairUnreadableError with its code, and the guidance says nothing was replaced; it used to collapse into 'damaged', whose advice to re-pair would have replaced a key that may have been fine. Proven by src/main/runtime/e2ee-keypair.test.ts (injected ENOSPC with firing proof; real EISDIR read fault).",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/runtime/e2ee-keypair.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The directory fsync after the rename is a no-op on Windows (see the comment in shared/secure-file.ts), so on Windows the barrier proves the file's contents reached disk before the rename, not that the rename itself survives a power loss.",
        "Nothing yet clears the matching device-registry entries when an identity is deliberately forgotten, so a re-pair leaves stale rows behind.",
        "The side-effect-free observer readE2EEPublicKey still answers null for both 'invalid' and 'could not be read'; only the loader, which decides guidance and replacement, keeps them apart."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mobile.user-drafts",
      "state": "AsyncStorage keys orca:user-draft:v1:<sha256(surface, host, worktree, subject)> — unsent chat composer text per tab, unsaved edits of an editable file preview (file-preview, keyed by host file path) and of an editable markdown doc (markdown-editor, keyed by the doc's relative path), and recognized dictation that reached no surface (dictation-salvage, per host and worktree)",
      "writer": "mobile/src/storage/draft-journal.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "The user's own unsent words. Before this contract they lived only in React state, so the OS killing the app discarded them with no notice, and recognized dictation whose target had gone was dropped outright. A key is replaced whole, so a reader sees the old draft or the new one; the newest edit can be lost inside the 300 ms save debounce. Load keeps 'unreadable' (the corrupt entry is removed and the user told) apart from 'failed' (a storage error removes nothing), and a chat draft is cleared only after the host confirms the send. An editor surface journals its text against the hash of the content it loaded, retires the entry when a save moves the saved content, the text is edited back to it, or the user explicitly discards it (the file preview Discard, the markdown Discard Changes and dirty Refresh, and Discard & Leave, each cancelling the pending write so the unmount flush cannot re-save it), and restores it only as a dirty draft, never saved, when the reopened content hashes the same; a read that resolves after the editor moved to another document is dropped rather than applied to it, a draft whose base changed is neither applied nor deleted, and an unreadable or failed read is never treated as no draft. The editor surfaces are wired with no new UI, so their stale-base, unreadable and failed outcomes are not shown to the user.",
      "provenBy": [
        "mobile/src/storage/draft-journal.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/session/use-mobile-native-chat-drafts-persistence.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/files/use-mobile-file-preview-draft-journal.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/files/MobileFilePreviewScreen.draft-journal.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/session/use-mobile-markdown-doc-draft-journal.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/session/markdown-doc-draft-journal-route-source.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/dictation/mobile-dictation-transcript-dispatch.test.ts"
      ],
      "knownGaps": [
        "No real process-death run yet: persistence is proven by remounting the hook over the same storage, not by killing the app on an emulator.",
        "A stale-base editor draft (the file changed on the host after the edit) is kept but nothing shows it: there is no retrieval UI, which is a product decision for the Owner.",
        "A later edit of the same file supersedes a stale-base entry, since the journal holds one entry per file; the stale text is preserved at restore time but not protected against the next edit.",
        "An editor emptied to blank text is not journaled: the journal refuses empty text, so emptying a file or doc is not durable.",
        "An older app build drops a record whose surface it does not know (the schema check fails and the entry is removed), so a downgrade loses file-preview and markdown-editor drafts.",
        "The markdown editor's restore is pinned on the hook and, for the session route, on a source check that the route calls it, because the route is too large to render in a unit test.",
        "Salvaged dictation is kept and the user told, but no screen yet lists or re-inserts it.",
        "Host removal purges drafts outside the per-key mutation queue, so a save in flight at that instant can recreate one entry."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime.device-registry",
      "state": "<userData>/orca-devices.json — every paired device token, scope, reach, relay binding. A bare JSON array, so an older build still reads it.",
      "writer": "src/main/runtime/device-registry.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "Every pairing at once: the file was published by a rename with no fsync, so a power loss could leave it empty or torn, and the loader then treated an unreadable file as no devices. The writer now fsyncs before the rename, and an unparseable file is preserved as orca-devices.json.corrupt-<ms> while the registry fails closed; an I/O read failure leaves the file in place and the registry unavailable.",
      "onFailure": "save() does not catch: the storage error reaches the caller with its code. A grant (addDevice, rotatePendingDevice, a widened pairing reach) persists before the in-memory swap, so a grant whose write fails is never valid and pairing reports the error. A revoke (removeDevice) swaps memory first, so a failed write can never leave the device valid; the error still propagates and the next successful save persists the removal. Proven by src/main/runtime/device-registry-durability.test.ts (V-REV-GRANT-ORDER, V-REV-SAVE-FAILURE, injected rename failure).",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/runtime/device-registry-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The directory fsync after the rename is a no-op on Windows (see shared/secure-file.ts), so on Windows the proven claim is that the contents reached disk before the rename, not that the rename itself survives a power loss.",
        "The forget-identity cleanup is deferred (see runtime.e2ee-host-identity)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime.device-revocations",
      "state": "<userData>/orca-device-revocations.json — sha256 digests of the tokens of devices the owner removed, with the removal time",
      "writer": "src/main/runtime/device-registry.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "Only the refusal reason: a revoked token reads unknown_token instead of device_revoked, and is still refused because its device row is gone from orca-devices.json. A missing or unreadable file never makes the registry unavailable and never authorizes anyone. Devices are written first, so a failed write here can lose a reason but never resurrect a device.",
      "onFailure": "The tombstone write runs inside save() after the device write and throws to the caller; tombstonesDirty stays set, so the next successful save writes the tombstones again. The device row is already gone from memory and from orca-devices.json, so its token stays refused. Proven by src/main/runtime/device-registry-durability.test.ts (V-REV-TOMBSTONE-SAVE-FAILURE: a directory in place of the file makes the write fail).",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/runtime/device-registry-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The directory fsync after the rename is a no-op on Windows (see shared/secure-file.ts).",
        "The file is not in MOBILE_PAIRING_USERDATA_FILES: the userData migration needs every listed file to exist, and this one legitimately may not."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "speech.openai-api-key",
      "state": "~/.orca/openai-speech-token.enc — the safeStorage-encrypted OpenAI speech key",
      "writer": "src/main/speech/openai-api-key-store.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "Losing the newest write costs one re-paste of the key. The failure that mattered was tearing, not loss: the writer was a bare writeFileSync, and the reader pair splits on a fragment — hasOpenAiSpeechApiKey() tests existence only, so a truncated file reported the key as configured while every transcription failed to decrypt. It now stages and renames, so the file is the old key or the new one.",
      "knownGaps": [
        "No test drives a torn write. The atomicity is inherited from writeFileDurableSync rather than pinned here, so a future refactor back to a direct write would not go red at this contract.",
        "A key written while safeStorage is unavailable is stored in plaintext at mode 0600; that is a confidentiality decision, not a durability one, and is unchanged by this contract."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "speech.model-cache",
      "state": "Downloaded sherpa-onnx speech model files under the shared models directory",
      "writer": "src/main/speech/model-manager.ts",
      "tier": "regenerable",
      "lostIf": "Nothing the user cannot rebuild: each catalog file carries a sha256 and size, so a lost or partial model is re-downloaded and re-verified. A partially written model must never be loaded, which the status machine enforces by publishing 'ready' only after verification rather than after the write.",
      "knownGaps": [
        "Model bytes are large enough that an fsync-per-file would cost an I/O storm for state the catalog can reproduce, which is why this is regenerable rather than atomic-only."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "persistence.profile-data-file",
      "state": "orca-data.json and its .bak ring — workspace session, terminal layouts, open files, and each SSH target's host-key pin (sshTargets[].hostKeyPin)",
      // Two publishers, and the gate holds both to the tier. The transfer writer was a bare
      // write+rename while this contract read as satisfied — a contract names state, not one file,
      // and its guarantee is only as strong as its weakest publisher.
      "writer": [
        "src/main/persistence/loading-store/store.ts",
        "src/main/persistence/loading-store/unreadable-profile-quarantine.ts",
        "src/main/orca-profiles/profile-project-state-file.ts"
      ],
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "A power loss between rename and flush leaves a zero-length profile, and the user reopens Orca to an empty workspace.",
      "onFailure": "A failed write never touches the previous file. The store writes a temp sibling, fsyncs it and renames only afterwards, and the transfer writer does the same through writeFileDurableSync; on any failure the temp file is removed, so orca-data.json stays byte-identical, and the store advances its written-hash and durable generation only after the rename, so the next save or flush republishes the pending state instead of skipping it as unchanged. Callers that act on durability are told: flushOrThrow throws a DurablePublishError (stage and OS code kept, ENOSPC intact) and flushPendingOrThrowAsync rejects with the raw OS error. That reaches the session:flush RPC; terminal-binding persistence, which restores the in-memory session, shuts the newly spawned PTY down and answers with product copy; the Codex reset-credit ledger, which restores the previous ledger; runtime tab retirement, which returns an explicit unpersisted null and publishes nothing; profile switch and project transfer, which refuse to proceed over an unflushed profile; and the renderer unload checkpoint, which answers {ok:false} so a navigating path aborts. A profile transfer in move mode writes the target first and the source second, so a failure at the second write rejects with the project present in both profiles: duplicated, never lost. Not reported to anyone, by design or for lack of a surface: the debounced background save only logs and no timer re-arms it, so the pending state waits for the next mutation, flush or quit; flush(), used by the SSH-target, SSH-lease and automation operations, logs and returns normally; the quit flush (flushAsync) and the update-install flush (flushPendingAsync) swallow because teardown has no surface to tell; the auth-restart preservation step logs and continues the restart; and the beforeunload write (session:set-sync) answers true after a failed flush and its caller discards the value. In every unreported case the loss is bounded to edits since the last successful save and the previous file stays intact. The load side has its own failure semantics: a primary that cannot be read or parsed is copied byte-identical to orca-data.json.unreadable-<UTC stamp> (exclusive create, short-write loop, fsynced, never replacing an earlier quarantine; a copy that cannot be completed is removed and reported as unquarantined with the OS code, never as ok) before any backup is restored over it or any default is saved. A profile that parses but whose whole workspaceSession, a whole per-host partition in workspaceSessionsByHostId, or that partition map itself fails validation keeps the rest of the profile, and is defaulted only after one such copy of the whole original file per load, however many parts were defaulted (a later throw in the same load reuses that copy). Store.getStartupLoadOutcome() names the result (loaded, absent, recovered-from-backup with the slot, unreadable-defaulted with the quarantine path, session-unreadable-defaulted with the quarantine path and the defaulted parts, or unreadable-unquarantined and session-unreadable-unquarantined with the OS code when the copy itself failed, in which case the load continues exactly as it did before). Proven by src/main/persisted-session-unreadable-load.test.ts, src/main/persistence/loading-store/unreadable-profile-quarantine.test.ts (the short-write and full-disk cases at the fs boundary), src/main/persistence-durable-publish-order.test.ts, src/main/persistence-write-failure-semantics.test.ts and src/main/orca-profiles/profile-project-transfer-write-failure.test.ts.",
      "provenBy": [
        "src/main/persistence-async-write-syscalls.test.ts",
        "src/main/persistence-durable-publish-order.test.ts",
        "src/main/persistence-write-failure-semantics.test.ts",
        "src/main/persisted-session-unreadable-load.test.ts",
        "src/main/orca-profiles/profile-project-state-durability.test.ts",
        "src/main/orca-profiles/profile-project-transfer-write-failure.test.ts"
      ],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The .bak ring bounds loss at roughly one hour, so the contract is 'no total loss', not 'no loss'.",
        "A pin lost with a newest write or a restored .bak degrades to unpinned: the next interactive connect asks again, and an automatic reconnect follows the legacy unpinned grace (counted, never pinned). Loss never turns into trust in a changed key, and a damaged pin is refused as unverifiable rather than read as no pin.",
        "A quarantine is never pruned and no UI offers it: an unreadable profile leaves orca-data.json.unreadable-* files beside the profile for support to read, one per corruption event. A valid-JSON profile whose whole workspaceSession, whole per-host partition or partition map fails validation is quarantined once per load before the default lands (a single corrupt tab entry is still salvaged and the drop logged, with no quarantine); a corrupt whole workspaceSession does not itself schedule a save, so each relaunch before any save writes another quarantine of the same bytes (bounded by the next save).",
        "Only loading-store rotates the .bak ring; a profile transfer replaces the file with no backup refresh, so a transfer is the one publish with no recovery slot behind it.",
        "A failed debounced save is not retried by a timer (pinned by persistence-write-failure-semantics.test.ts): if the disk recovers while the app is idle, the pending state stays unwritten until the next mutation, flush or quit.",
        "The unreported paths named in onFailure (debounced save, flush(), quit, update-install, auth-restart, beforeunload) have no user surface; the contract bounds their loss and does not remove it.",
        "Rejections from flushOrThrow, flushPendingOrThrowAsync and the transfer cross IPC as raw error text with no product-state classification; how each renderer caller words them is not measured here, so the copy pass owns it.",
        "Observed against a real full ext4 volume on Linux through config/scripts/real-enospc-volume.sh and the opt-in src/main/persistence-real-enospc.test.ts (ENOSPC at write for the async flush, the sync flush and the transfer's target write, previous file byte-identical, no temp file, publishes after space is freed); Windows full-disk mapping is not exercised."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "daemon.terminal-history-checkpoint",
      "state": "checkpoint.json per daemon session, plus the generation-headered output.log it supersedes",
      "writer": "src/main/daemon/terminal-history-session-writer.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "The checkpoint is the sole copy once the log is reset. An unsynced publish can expose a zero-length checkpoint whose increments were already discarded, losing the session's scrollback across a crash the daemon was built to survive.",
      "onFailure": "HistoryManager.checkpoint returns 'failed' when the durable write throws, distinct from 'unavailable' (no writer, or a disabled session). The adapter's five flag-clearing sites ask checkpointDidNotLand(), so a failed checkpoint keeps the session dirty and answers 'deferred' instead of 'done'. The OS error reaches the onWriteError channel with its code intact. Proven by src/main/daemon/history-checkpoint-storage-failure.test.ts.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/daemon/terminal-history-checkpoint-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The log reset is ordered after the checkpoint fsync, but the reset itself is not fsynced; a loss there costs the reset, not the checkpoint."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "terminal.scrollback-snapshot",
      "state": "terminal-scrollback/<ref>.bin, referenced by scrollbackRefsByLeafId in the profile data file",
      "writer": "src/main/terminal-scrollback-snapshots.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "The referencing profile file is fsynced and the blob was not, so a durable pointer can outlive its referent. The reader returns an empty string for a zero-length snapshot, so the loss surfaces as empty scrollback rather than as a failure.",
      "onFailure": "The writers return a closed outcome — stored | nothing-to-store | not-stored{failure, code, stage} — so a full disk (storage-full, ENOSPC) is distinguishable from an empty buffer and from an unreachable snapshot root. Both migration paths keep the buffer inline on any non-stored outcome, so a storage failure costs the blob, never the scrollback. Proven by src/main/terminal-scrollback-snapshot-enospc.test.ts.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/terminal-scrollback-snapshot-durability.test.ts"],
      "knownGaps": [
        "The reader still cannot distinguish a missing snapshot from a genuinely empty one; see shared/terminal-snapshot-unavailability.ts for the vocabulary the live wire path already uses."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "conversation.orca-owned-record",
      "state": "conversations/<conversationId>.json — the Orca-owned conversation: its minted identity, title, optional agent binding, provider provenance, turn lifecycle and product-visible messages. It deliberately carries no project of its own: the agent owns that relation, because only PersistedState can take part in Project id succession",
      "writer": "src/main/conversation/conversation-store.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "This is the only copy. The provider transcript is evidence, not a substitute: it belongs to the provider, it rotates, the provider may delete it, and it carries no Orca conversation identity — so a lost record cannot be reconstructed from it, only guessed at. Losing one costs the conversation as a product entity: its id, anything later bound to that id, and the ability to reopen it at all.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/conversation/conversation-store-syscall-proof.test.ts"],
      "onFailure": "Never reported as success. conversation-store does not catch writeFileDurable, so ENOSPC/EACCES reject: conversations:create and createForAgent reject to the renderer with the OS error message; during a turn, conversation-turn-runner catches it and returns outcome 'failed' with that message as the reason, after trying to mark unsettled turns failed (if that second write also fails, the IPC call rejects instead). The prior record is kept, because the temp file is renamed over it only after its fsync. Weakness: the user sees the raw OS message, not product copy.",
      "knownGaps": [
        "Reachable since W3: src/main/ipc/conversations.ts calls the service and src/preload/index.ts exposes conversations:create, so a user with the Conversations surface enabled writes real records. This contract now describes state users have, not a capability. The surface is still opt-in (showConversationsButton), so the population is small rather than absent.",
        "No backup ring. Unlike the profile file there is no .bak behind a conversation, so a record that fails to parse is refused rather than recovered; the bytes are preserved on disk for a later repair, but nothing rotates a known-good copy forward.",
        "Never exercised against a real power loss, and no real-app run: the syscall-order proof counts the barrier at the module boundary under vitest."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "backlog.intent-ledger-record",
      "state": "backlog/items/<itemId>.json and backlog/candidates/<candidateId>.json under the active profile directory — the Universal Backlog intent ledger: accepted intent, its source links, evidence, lifecycle state and append-only event log, one file per record. Records carry schemaVersion 1 unless they hold a v2-only field (the candidate hint-assigned event), and this build reads the range 1..2, so older builds keep reading every record that needs nothing newer",
      "writer": "src/main/backlog/backlog-record-file.ts",
      "tier": "crash-durable",
      "lostIf": "Accepted intent is the only copy. The chats it was harvested from rotate or vanish, which is the product's reason to exist, so a lost record cannot be reconstructed from its sources and the intent is simply gone.",
      "provenBy": [
        "src/main/backlog/backlog-store-syscall-proof.test.ts",
        "src/main/backlog/backlog-store-durability.test.ts",
        "src/main/backlog/backlog-record-directory-sync.test.ts"
      ],
      "onFailure": "Every publish returns a closed storage-failed outcome naming the stage and the OS error code instead of rejecting. The prior record is kept because the rename follows the fsync, so a failed write leaves the last published bytes in place.",
      "knownGaps": [
        "No backup ring or repair tool: a record that fails to parse is quarantined with its bytes untouched, but nothing rotates a known-good copy forward.",
        "Orphan temp files from a death between write and rename are not swept; removeStaleDurableWriteTempFiles works per final path.",
        "Never exercised against a real power loss, and no real-app run: the syscall-order proof counts the barrier at the module boundary under vitest.",
        "Directory fsync is skipped where the platform cannot open a directory for it (Windows), so the rename-recorded-but-not-persisted window is not closed there; the same applies to the backlog/ and items/ or candidates/ entries a first capture creates, which are fsynced in their parents elsewhere.",
        "The cross-process fence is narrowed, not eliminated: the revision is re-read synchronously before the rename, not held under a lock.",
        "A Windows rename blocked by another handle surfaces as storage-failed at the publish stage with no retry.",
        "A new record publishes by hard link so a taken name fails EEXIST instead of being replaced; a profile on a filesystem without hard links (FAT, exFAT, some network shares) fails every create as storage-failed at the publish stage.",
        "Records are plaintext at rest in the profile directory.",
        "Evidence and link counts are capped per item with a 32 KiB state headroom, but the event log itself is unbounded: an item whose events alone fill the byte cap refuses every further write as record-too-large.",
        "Reachable only through runtime RPC until the Phase 2 surface lands."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "browser.session-meta",
      "state": "browser-session-meta.json — browser profiles, default source, pending cookie imports",
      "writer": "src/main/browser/browser-session-meta-store.ts",
      "tier": "atomic-only",
      "lostIf": "Losing the newest write costs one profile edit. The severe failure here was never durability: a read-modify-write seeded from a failed read published an erasure of every profile, so the contract this file owes is a classified read, not an fsync.",
      "provenBy": ["src/main/browser/browser-session-meta-transient-read-failure.test.ts"],
